        "avx512vpopcntdq",
        "avx512vnni",
        "avx512bitalg",
        "avx512bf16",
        "avx512vp2intersect",
        "avx512fp16"
      ],
      "note": "Each library path must check its exact required subset and OS state; AVX512F alone is not the entire family."
    },
    {
      "name": "FMA3",
      "rust_features": [
        "fma"
      ],
      "note": "Separate requirement; do not infer FMA3 from AVX2 or confuse it with FMA4."
    },
    {
      "name": "SHA",
      "rust_features": [
        "sha"
      ],
      "note": "x86 SHA instruction support is separate from the ARM SHA extensions."
    }
  ],
  "rules": [
    "Preserve each OS/target ABI baseline. Do not copy Linux x86 or ARM baseline flags blindly onto Windows or Apple targets.",
    "Portable release builds must not use target-cpu=native, -march=native, -mcpu=native or globally force optional SIMD/crypto features beyond the declared target baseline.",
    "Delegate instruction selection to existing vetted Rust/native libraries and standard OS-aware CPU detection. Do not add a custom CPUID, assembler or SIMD wrapper layer.",
    "Global compiler feature flags can make Rust runtime feature macros constant true; a runtime-looking check cannot repair an already raised binary baseline.",
    "Inventory Rust, C/C++, build scripts, inherited environment, compiler builtins, native SDKs, transitive dependencies and bundled libraries before qualification.",
    "Optimized target-specific builds must declare their CPU profile and must not be presented as universal architecture packages.",
    "Qualify both supported acceleration and feature-absent fallback against the same independent functional/crypto/media oracles. Require no SIGILL and no behavior regression.",
    "Do not claim compatibility from a disassembly mnemonic scan, compiler cfg, CPUID masking or headless startup alone. Verify the instruction-enforcement control and representative actual hardware.",
    "Keep ARM64 feature names, platform baselines, native CPU/page size and library intake requirements separate; x86 SIMD names do not map directly to ARM."
  ],
